  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1026 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThis same pattern will continue through mid-week with daily chances \nof convection favoring areas on the caprock. Tuesday evening into \nthe nighttime hours will see the highest chances of precipitation \nthrough the week. This is mostly in part due to a cold front that \nwill be dropping south through the Texas Panhandle. This will create \nan area of significant low level ascent beneath a moist mid-level \natmosphere. Areas on the caprock will see the highest chances of \nconvection with this area in closest proximity to the mid-level \nmoisture and low level ascent. Heavy rain may be the most \nsignificant threat from this activity with precipitable water values \nover the 95th percentile. The upper level ridge will begin to push \nback west on Wednesday starting to advect the mid-level moisture \nback to the north. As a result, convective chances will most likely \nbe limited to the western South Plains and extreme southwestern \nTexas Panhandle again. Although the ridge will be moving back closer \nto the region, temperatures will continue the downward trend with \nthe ridge weakening. Model guidance has not been consistent from \nFriday and beyond. Current blends paint a broad precipitation \nchances with flow aloft returning to the southwest and a few minor \nshort wave troughs potentially affecting the region. Additionally, a \ncold front may be added into the mix sometime next weekend.",
